package tau:extension@0.8.0;

/// ---- 0.8.0: subtraction (draft for review, 2026-10-01) ----
///
/// STATUS: proposal. The contract in force is still `wit/tau.wit` (0.7.0);
/// this file becomes it only after review (docs/wit-redesign.md is the
/// 0.7.0 precedent for how a draft becomes the contract).
///
/// Three rulings, one direction: the contract keeps only what only it can do.
///
///   1. The per-capability runtime gates are gone, and so is `--deny-wasi`.
///      With ambient WASI granted (fs / env / stdio / args / network), an
///      origin allowlist or an argv approval was never a boundary: a component
///      that imports `wasi:sockets` or `wasi:filesystem` goes around the gated
///      interface (docs/extensions.md section 7, wit-review F1). A gate that is
///      not a wall costs every user the vocabulary and buys nothing. What is
///      left is ONE authorization act -- installing/trusting signed bytes --
///      and the declaration shown at that moment is read from the component
///      type, not hand-written. `types.error` therefore loses `refused`:
///      nothing refuses at call time any more, and a variant with no producer
///      is not a contract.
///   2. No component is a model. `world provider`, `world realtime`,
///      `interface models` and `interface session` are deleted. The model is
///      the harness's own brain; vendor wire formats (chat completions,
///      responses, messages, realtime sockets) are host code, which is also
///      where the media plane already lives. The line this draws is the useful
///      one: the set of models is closed and owned here, while the set of tool
///      protocols stays open and stays out of core (bridges).
///   3. Audio and video never cross this ABI. Device I/O, the clock, the
///      jitter buffer and playback belong to the host (docs/realtime-av.md red
///      line 1): a guest has no clock it can await, so a real-time deadline is
///      not something a component can meet -- a structural fact, not a
///      performance measurement. With the realtime world gone the media plane
///      is host-internal end to end; the subscription path keeps its audio arm
///      as a COUNT -- an observer can see that the assistant is speaking,
///      never hear it, which is the rule the bus already followed.
///
/// Net: four worlds become two (`extension`, `bridge`); `stream`/`future`
/// survive only on the bridge's I/O interfaces, so the component-model async
/// ABI stops being a property of "writing a tau extension" and becomes a
/// property of "talking to the outside world" -- which is where the non-Rust
/// language matrix is blocked today anyway (docs/wasm-languages.md).
///
/// What the deletion costs, stated rather than discovered:
///   * model coverage becomes release cadence -- a vendor tau does not ship
///     has no component path. The escape hatch is OpenAI-compatible
///     `--base-url` host config, not a new contract surface;
///   * a component runs with the permissions of the tau process, always.
///     Signing answers "which component is this", never "what may it do"; if a
///     real boundary is wanted, that is an OS-level question (account,
///     container, VM) about the process tau runs in.
///
/// ---- 0.7.0 (unchanged, still in force): the async / stream / resource
///      redesign (docs/wit-redesign.md) ----
///
/// Shape change only: the feature set is 0.6.0's, minus the parts whose only
/// reason to exist was the synchronous ABI.
///
///   * `u64` handles and their host-side generation table become `resource`.
///     Ownership replaces the whole "unknown handle / stale handle after a
///     trap rebuild" error family, and dropping a resource IS the close.
///   * Bounded-wait polling (`process.read-stdout(.., max, timeout-ms)`,
///     `http.read-body(.., max, timeout-ms)`, `ws.recv(handle, timeout-ms)`,
///     `process.write-stdin(..) -> taken`) becomes `stream<T>` and
///     `future<result<_, error>>`. Backpressure is the stream's capacity,
///     cancellation is dropping it, and no call parks a host thread -- so
///     "0 is rejected, because block-forever is not a contract" stops being
///     a contract question and becomes the transport's default.
///   * Payloads whose schema tau owns are typed -- probe points, probe
///     payloads and verdicts, notification levels, subscription topics. JSON
///     stays at the leaves tau does not own the schema for: tool arguments,
///     JSON Schema, extension-defined facts.
///   * Host failures are classified (`types.error`) instead of arriving as an
///     untyped string a guest must not pattern-match on.
///
/// Requires the Component Model async ABI on both sides (wasmtime 49+,
/// wit-bindgen 0.62+) for the worlds that carry streams and futures -- since
/// 0.8.0 that is the bridge world only. It does NOT require WASI 0.3: guests
/// stay `wasm32-wasip2` with `wasi:*@0.2.x` imports.
///
/// Deliberately NOT here (each is a capability, not a shape):
///   * a media plane in the guest (0.8.0 ruling 3 -- the host owns devices,
///     clocks and buffers);
///   * a guest that is the model (0.8.0 ruling 2 -- models are host code).
///     The re-entry condition is written down so the cost of reversal stays
///     bounded: if long-tail vendors or self-hosted gateways turn out to be
///     unreachable through the built-in providers plus the OpenAI-compatible
///     endpoint, the way back in is a SEPARATE package, not this one, so the
///     language matrix and the 1.0.0 contract freeze are not disturbed;
///   * streaming media sources (`media-source.stream`) -- the blob arm
///     already covers payloads too large to hold;
///   * streaming request bodies (`http.request` body as a stream);
///   * `process` env/cwd (the component inherits the host's environment and
///     cwd; argv is how a bridge names what it runs);
///   * a stream for `host.subscribe` (see the interface comment).

/// ---- Message trunk: tau owns this schema, so it is typed, not JSON ----
///
/// Content blocks and messages shared by the host channel (see
/// docs/host-channel.md). Convention (docs/extensions.md): payloads whose
/// schema tau owns are WIT types; JSON survives only at leaves tau does
/// not own the schema for (tool arguments, JSON Schema, extension-defined
/// facts).
interface types {
    /// Who produced a message. Mirrors tau_core::types::Role.
    enum role {
        /// The human (or the harness on their behalf).
        user,
        /// The model.
        assistant,
        /// A tool result message.
        tool,
    }

    /// Why a response ended. Mirrors tau_core::model::StopReason. Lives in
    /// `types` since 0.7.0: probe payloads need it too (`after-response`,
    /// `before-run-end`), and the interface that used to own it — `events` —
    /// is gone.
    enum stop-reason {
        /// The model finished its turn.
        stop,
        /// The model wants tool calls executed.
        tool-use,
        /// Hit the output limit.
        length,
        /// Ended by a failure (a prior `error` event says what).
        error,
        /// Cancelled from outside (e.g. a steering interrupt).
        aborted,
    }

    /// Why a host call failed.
    ///
    /// Two arms since 0.8.0: with the runtime consent gates gone there is no
    /// longer a call that fails because "nobody granted this", so `refused`
    /// had no producer left and left the contract with them. The arm is the
    /// part a guest branches on; the string is the detail meant for the human
    /// reading the log. Never match on the string.
    variant error {
        /// The call itself failed: network error, peer gone, child exited,
        /// program not found, permission denied by the OS, signature check
        /// failed. The detail is the host's or the OS's own message.
        failed(string),
        /// The call is not valid here: unknown topic, size limit, wrong
        /// state, already closed. A guest bug or a contract misuse.
        invalid(string),
    }

    /// A media payload. Mirrors tau_core::types::Media: the media type and
    /// where the bytes live, nothing else. Raw bytes cross the ABI — never
    /// base64 (that exists only at tau's JSON edges: session file, vendor
    /// HTTP).
    record media {
        /// MIME type, e.g. "image/png", "audio/pcm;rate=24000".
        media-type: string,
        /// Where the bytes live.
        source: media-source,
    }

    /// Where a media's bytes live. Mirrors tau_core::types::MediaSource.
    /// 0.7.0 considered a `stream(list<u8>)` arm for payloads too large to
    /// hold and left it out: the blob arm is content-addressed, already on
    /// disk, and does not make every carrier of `content` a stream owner.
    variant media-source {
        /// Raw bytes.
        bytes(list<u8>),
        /// Remote reference; the host passes it through when the model's API
        /// accepts URLs directly.
        url(string),
        /// Content-addressed blob store reference: "sha256:<hex>".
        blob(string),
    }

    /// An attachment that is not an image, audio or video clip. Mirrors
    /// tau_core::types::Content::File, whose filename field lives on this
    /// arm (as it does in Rust) rather than on `media`.
    record file {
        /// The file payload.
        media: media,
        /// Original filename, when known.
        name: option<string>,
    }

    /// A tool invocation requested by the assistant.
    record tool-call {
        /// Provider-assigned call id (echoed back by tool-result).
        id: string,
        /// Registered tool name.
        name: string,
        /// The one JSON leaf: model-produced arbitrary JSON arguments.
        arguments-json: string,
    }

    /// A block of a tool result: the non-recursive half of `content`.
    ///
    /// It has to be a separate variant, and that is a toolchain fact, not a
    /// style choice: `content` reaching itself through a tool result is
    /// refused — `wasm-tools component wit` answers
    /// `error: type `content` depends on itself` (a recursive `json` for
    /// arbitrary JSON fails the same way). Rust's
    /// `Content::ToolResult { content: Vec<Content> }` is therefore wider
    /// than the ABI; a tool result never carries a tool call or a nested
    /// result, so the narrowing is semantic, not lossy. The arms mirror
    /// `content`'s non-tool arms one for one, and keep mirroring them: the
    /// host converter matches exhaustively, so an arm added to one and not
    /// the other is a compile error, not a silent drift.
    variant result-block {
        /// A plain text block.
        text(string),
        /// An image (screenshot, photo, diagram).
        image(media),
        /// An audio clip.
        audio(media),
        /// A video clip.
        video(media),
        /// An arbitrary file attachment (PDF, archive, source bundle, ...).
        file(file),
    }

    /// The outcome of one tool call, as conversation content. Multi-block
    /// since 0.3.0 (docs/tool-media.md, wit-review F4); media bytes cross
    /// the ABI raw, never base64.
    record tool-result {
        /// The call this answers.
        call-id: string,
        /// Result blocks: text and/or media.
        content: list<result-block>,
        /// True when the tool reported failure.
        is-error: bool,
    }

    /// One block of a message's content. Mirrors tau_core::types::Content
    /// arm for arm — including the four media arms: a guest says which kind
    /// of medium it is sending, instead of the host inferring it from the
    /// MIME major type. (That inference, and its "naming an image is a
    /// validation error" rule, are gone: the arm is the intent.)
    variant content {
        /// A plain text block.
        text(string),
        /// An image (screenshot, photo, diagram).
        image(media),
        /// An audio clip.
        audio(media),
        /// A video clip.
        video(media),
        /// An arbitrary file attachment (PDF, archive, source bundle, ...).
        file(file),
        /// A tool invocation requested by the assistant.
        tool-call(tool-call),
        /// The outcome of one tool call.
        tool-result(tool-result),
    }

    /// One message in the conversation: a role plus ordered blocks.
    record message {
        /// Who produced it.
        role: role,
        /// Ordered blocks: text, media, tool calls/results.
        content: list<content>,
    }
}

/// Tools an extension contributes to the agent.
interface tools {
    use types.{result-block};

    /// A tool the model may call, described with a JSON Schema parameter object.
    record definition {
        name: string,
        description: string,
        /// JSON Schema for the tool's arguments, serialized. JSON because
        /// JSON Schema is itself a schema language tau does not own.
        parameters-json: string,
    }

    record tool-result {
        /// Result blocks handed back to the model: text and/or a media
        /// block (image/audio/video/file — multi-block since 0.3.0,
        /// docs/tool-media.md). The call-id is paired host-side, the guest
        /// never sees it.
        content: list<result-block>,
        is-error: bool,
    }

    /// Called once at load time to enumerate the tools this component
    /// provides.
    ///
    /// `async` since 0.7.0, and it is not decoration: a bridge whose tool
    /// list lives on a remote server (examples/mcp-bridge) has to perform
    /// its handshake HERE, and a synchronously lowered export cannot wait
    /// for anything. Measured, not assumed — `block_on` inside a sync
    /// export traps with "cannot block a synchronous task before
    /// returning" (docs/wit-redesign.md §5, leg 5). An extension whose
    /// list is a literal awaits nothing and reads exactly as it did.
    definitions: async func() -> list<definition>;

    /// Execute one tool call. `arguments-json` is the model-produced JSON
    /// object matching the definition's schema.
    ///
    /// `async` since 0.7.0: a tool that talks to the host (http, process,
    /// ws) awaits those calls in place instead of parking a host thread
    /// inside a synchronous guest invocation. The result is still whole —
    /// tool *progress* streaming is a separate feature and is not smuggled
    /// in through a stream of result blocks.
    execute: async func(name: string, arguments-json: string) -> tool-result;
}

/// Probes: lifecycle points where an extension can observe and influence a
/// run. See docs/probes.md for the point list and payload shapes.
/// (Named `hooks` in 0.1.0; renamed to match the code, the docs, and the
/// `tau probes` CLI — wit-review F7.)
///
/// Typed since 0.7.0. 0.6.0 passed `point: string` + `payload-json: string`:
/// the point set and every payload shape are tau's, documented in
/// docs/probes.md, so that was JSON on ground tau owns. A guest that trims
/// context no longer parses the message trunk to do it.
///
/// Still synchronous on purpose: the harness pauses the loop and waits for
/// the verdict, so a probe is a decision point, not an I/O opportunity. An
/// `async` probe would let a component hold the run open on a network call;
/// probes that need I/O should return quickly and do the I/O in a tool.
interface probes {
    use types.{message, stop-reason, tool-call, tool-result, result-block};
    use tools.{definition};

    /// The probe points, named as docs/probes.md names them (the WIT arm is
    /// kebab-case, the host's string form is snake_case: `before-tool` is
    /// `before_tool` on the bus and in `tau probes`).
    enum point {
        /// 1. The user's prompt, before the run starts.
        before-run,
        /// 2. The assembled context (system prompt + messages).
        transform-context,
        /// 3. The final request: system, messages, tools.
        before-request,
        /// 4. The assembled assistant message and its stop reason.
        after-response,
        /// 5. A tool call, before it runs.
        before-tool,
        /// 6. A tool call's outcome.
        after-tool,
        /// 7. The run's produced messages and stop reason.
        before-run-end,
        /// 8. The message set a summarizer is about to see.
        before-compaction,
        /// 9. A branch navigation, before it happens.
        before-navigation,
        /// Observe-only: a session was created.
        session-start,
        /// Observe-only: a branch navigation was applied.
        branch,
        /// Observe-only: the session ended cleanly.
        session-end,
    }

    /// The assembled context of point 2; `replace` may return either field
    /// changed (`system: none` means "leave the system prompt alone").
    record assembled-context {
        system: option<string>,
        messages: list<message>,
    }

    /// The final request of point 3, after context assembly.
    record final-request {
        system: option<string>,
        messages: list<message>,
        tools: list<definition>,
    }

    /// Point 4: the message the model produced and why it stopped.
    record assembled-response {
        message: message,
        stop: stop-reason,
    }

    /// Point 6: the call as the model asked for it, and what came back.
    record tool-outcome {
        call: tool-call,
        content: list<result-block>,
        is-error: bool,
    }

    /// Point 7: what the run produced.
    record run-end {
        messages: list<message>,
        stop: stop-reason,
    }

    /// Point 8. Reason values are the host's ("manual", "auto"), mirrored
    /// from tau_core's compaction entry.
    record compaction {
        reason: string,
        messages: list<message>,
    }

    /// Point 9: `target` is an entry id, `summary` its first line (what the
    /// renderer would show).
    record navigation {
        target: string,
        summary: string,
    }

    /// The observe-only points: same shape for `session-start`/`session-end`,
    /// and `branch` names both ends of the navigation.
    record session-facts {
        session: string,
        cwd: string,
        model: string,
    }

    /// The entry navigated away from (the host's `from`; `from` is a WIT
    /// keyword, so the field cannot carry the name), and the entry moved to.
    record branch {
        previous: option<string>,
        to: string,
    }

    /// One arm per point, each carrying that point's payload. The host
    /// validates the pairing: a `before-tool` probe cannot answer with a
    /// `navigation` payload (that is `invalid`).
    variant payload {
        before-run(message),
        transform-context(assembled-context),
        before-request(final-request),
        after-response(assembled-response),
        before-tool(tool-call),
        after-tool(tool-outcome),
        before-run-end(run-end),
        before-compaction(compaction),
        before-navigation(navigation),
        session-start(session-facts),
        branch(branch),
        session-end(session-facts),
    }

    /// The answer to a probe. `block` carries the reason handed back to the
    /// model or the user; on the three observe-only points a `block` is
    /// reported on the bus as ignored, never honored (docs/probes.md).
    variant verdict {
        /// No opinion; the run proceeds unchanged.
        continue,
        /// Use this payload instead of the probed value — the same arm the
        /// probe was called with, with the fields that changed replaced.
        replace(payload),
        /// Veto the action; the reason is the whole answer.
        block(string),
    }

    /// Probe points this component handles, called once at load time.
    /// Empty list = this component observes nothing.
    points: func() -> list<point>;

    /// Synchronous probe call: point and payload in, verdict out. The
    /// harness is paused, so handlers on the hot path must be fast.
    probe: func(point: point, payload: payload) -> verdict;
}


/// The asynchronous half of a bridge's lifecycle.
///
/// `probes.probe` is synchronous and stays that way: for an extension it is a
/// decision point on the agent's own hot path, and an `async` probe would let
/// a component hold the whole run open on a network call. A bridge is where
/// that reasoning lands differently, because its obligations sit on exactly
/// those points: an IM adapter opens its long connection at `session-start`
/// and posts the platform's reply at `after-response`, and since 0.7.0 both
/// are `async func`s (`ws.connect`, `http.request`) that a synchronously
/// lowered export cannot await at all — measured, and not fixable by
/// spawning: a task spawned from a sync-lowered export is never polled
/// (docs/wit-redesign.md section 5, legs 1b/1c).
///
/// So the host calls `turn` right after `probe` has answered for the same
/// point, with the same payload, in a context where the component may await.
/// A bridge with nothing to do at a point returns; a bridge whose I/O lives
/// elsewhere (mcp-bridge does all of it in `definitions`) exports an empty
/// body. The sync probe stays the decision, exactly as before.
interface bridge-io {
    use probes.{point, payload};

    /// One probe call point, asynchronously. It returns nothing on purpose:
    /// decisions are `probe`'s verdict, and what a bridge learns here it acts
    /// on itself (steer, notify, post).
    turn: async func(point: point, payload: payload);
}

/// Guest→host channel: an extension's active return path. Facts
/// (notify/emit) are always available; the decision pair (steer/follow-up)
/// writes into the user's conversation, which is the one authority ambient
/// WASI cannot imitate -- since 0.8.0 it rides the install record, not a
/// per-run flag. Every call enqueues -- none re-enters the agent loop
/// synchronously; delivery follows the control channel's existing checkpoint
/// rules (steer lands after the in-flight tool results of the current turn,
/// follow-up when the run finishes). See docs/host-channel.md 语义红线.
interface host {
    use types.{message, content, error};

    /// Severity of a notification. Typed since 0.7.0 (0.6.0 passed the
    /// string "info" | "warn" | "error" and said so in a comment).
    enum level {
        info,
        warn,
        error,
    }

    /// The topics a subscription can carry (docs/stream-subscribe.md).
    /// Typed since 0.7.0: an unknown topic used to be a string the host had
    /// to refuse at call time, and is now unrepresentable.
    ///
    /// Both arms survive 0.8.0 with different meanings: no guest produces
    /// audio any more (ruling 3), so `audio-delta` is observe-only and
    /// count-only -- an extension may learn that the assistant is speaking
    /// and for how long, never hear it.
    enum topic {
        text-delta,
        audio-delta,
    }

    /// An assistant audio segment, counted. Since 0.8.0 the host is the only
    /// producer of assistant audio (no guest sits in the media path), and the
    /// bytes still never travel this channel: they land in the assistant
    /// message as content, and an observer sees the count and the media type.
    record audio-segment {
        /// How many bytes arrived in this segment.
        bytes: u64,
        /// The media type of the segment they belong to.
        media-type: string,
    }

    /// One high-frequency stream event drained by `poll`.
    variant stream-event {
        /// The subscription's ring overran: n events were dropped
        /// before the next event in this batch.
        lagged(u64),
        /// A fragment of assistant text.
        text-delta(string),
        /// An assistant audio segment, counted -- never the bytes.
        audio-delta(audio-segment),
    }

    /// A subscription. A resource since 0.7.0: the handle's generation
    /// table, the "unknown handle" error family and the explicit
    /// unsubscribe all collapse into ownership — dropping it unsubscribes.
    ///
    /// Deliberately NOT a `stream<stream-event>` (the redesign's own
    /// direction elsewhere): the bus publisher is the agent loop, and a
    /// stream would make the run's progress hostage to the slowest guest —
    /// the exact stall the bounded ring plus `lagged` exists to prevent.
    /// `lagged` is not an artifact of polling; it is the honest answer to
    /// "the guest was not reading", and it survives either carrier.
    resource subscription {
        /// Non-blocking drain of the backlog (empty when nothing
        /// accumulated). The ring holds 1024 events (mirroring the bus);
        /// on overrun the batch starts with lagged(n).
        poll: func() -> list<stream-event>;
    }

    /// User-visible notification; the renderer draws text blocks and a
    /// placeholder for media blocks. Not added to model history.
    notify: func(level: level, content: list<content>) -> result<_, error>;

    /// Publish an extension-defined fact to the event bus (observe-only).
    /// The fact's schema is external to tau, so this stays a JSON leaf;
    /// it must still be well-formed JSON, or the result says so.
    emit: func(event-json: string) -> result<_, error>;

    /// Steer the current run: the message is queued into the control
    /// channel and lands at the next checkpoint. The host validates
    /// role==user and the size limit and reports violations; it does not ask
    /// again at call time -- this authority is part of the install record
    /// (0.8.0: the one capability ambient WASI cannot imitate).
    steer: func(message: message) -> result<_, error>;

    /// Queue a message to run after the agent finishes. Same validation as
    /// steer.
    follow-up: func(message: message) -> result<_, error>;

    /// Subscribe to high-frequency stream events (docs/stream-subscribe.md
    /// — F2's high-frequency leg). Guests pull — the host never calls into
    /// a component asynchronously on this path — so the drain happens
    /// inside the guest's own invocations and the subscription's
    /// granularity is the guest's own call frequency. Outside a run (e.g.
    /// during definitions) the bus is not wired and subscribe fails.
    subscribe: func(topics: list<topic>) -> result<subscription, error>;
}

/// Spawn-with-pipes: a generic host capability for bridge components.
/// NOT linked for ordinary extensions: a tool that reads and writes files
/// has no reason to start processes, so the extension world does not import
/// this at all (0.8.0: the world boundary is the declaration; there is no
/// per-command gate behind it any more).
///
/// Resource-based since 0.7.0, and that is most of the redesign's point:
/// every `timeout-ms` parameter, the short-write accounting of
/// `write-stdin` ("taken, not delivered" — a blocking write that times out
/// cannot say how much it delivered, wit-review F12) and the handle
/// generation table all existed because a synchronous call cannot say
/// "not yet". A stream says it by waiting, and the guest's own await is
/// where a deadline belongs.
interface process {
    use types.{error};

    record options {
        /// argv[0] is the program; the host resolves it on PATH and applies
        /// its own environment policy (env and cwd are not contract fields:
        /// the component inherits the host's).
        argv: list<string>,
    }

    record exit-status {
        /// Exit code, when the process exited on its own.
        code: option<u32>,
        /// Signal that killed it, on platforms that have signals.
        signal: option<string>,
    }

    resource child {
        /// Spawn and return the child. Fails when the program cannot be
        /// started (not found, permission denied).
        spawn: static func(options: options) -> result<child, error>;

        /// The child's stdin, as a stream the guest writes and the host
        /// pumps. Dropping the writable end closes the pipe (EOF at the
        /// child); the future resolves when the host has passed everything
        /// on or the pipe broke, and its error says which.
        stdin: func(data: stream<u8>) -> future<result<_, error>>;

        /// The child's stdout, from the moment it is called (once per
        /// child). The stream ends at EOF — and also when the child dies,
        /// which is why the exit status comes from `wait`, not from the
        /// stream's end.
        stdout: func() -> stream<u8>;

        /// The child's stderr, same contract as `stdout`.
        stderr: func() -> stream<u8>;

        /// Resolves when the child exits. Asking twice is not an error;
        /// both futures resolve with the same status.
        wait: func() -> future<exit-status>;

        /// Terminate the child (its whole process tree) and release it.
        /// Idempotent.
        kill: func() -> result<_, error>;
    }
}

/// HTTP requests: the network capability bridge components use. Requests
/// go where the component says; redirects are never followed, because a
/// redirect is a different endpoint than the one the component asked for
/// (and the protocols here -- MCP streamable HTTP, platform APIs -- name
/// their endpoint explicitly). Plain data, resource-based, same shape as
/// `process` -- no wasi:http dependency.
interface http {
    use types.{error};

    /// A started response. Metadata is available as soon as `request`
    /// returns (headers have arrived); the body is the stream.
    resource response {
        /// HTTP status code.
        status: func() -> u16;

        /// One response header value (e.g. content-type, mcp-session-id).
        header: func(name: string) -> option<string>;

        /// The response body, consumed incrementally. SSE streams are read
        /// the same way as any other body: read what has arrived, stop
        /// reading when done — dropping the stream abandons the rest and
        /// closes the connection, which is the only cancellation an SSE
        /// consumer needs. The host keeps its own idle policy on a silent
        /// peer and reports it by ending the stream.
        body: func() -> stream<u8>;
    }

    /// Start a request, returning once the response headers are in. `method`
    /// is passed through verbatim -- send uppercase ("GET", "POST"); servers
    /// are case-sensitive about method names. The body is a value, not a
    /// stream: tau's requests carry MCP JSON-RPC messages and OAuth token
    /// calls, none of which are large enough to be worth a second
    /// direction of streaming.
    request: async func(method: string, url: string, headers: list<tuple<string, string>>, body: list<u8>) -> result<response, error>;
}

/// WebSocket connections: the frame-pipe capability bridge components use
/// for stream-mode protocols (IM long connections -- docs/im-channels.md).
/// The host is a pipe only: it never parses payloads.
interface ws {
    use types.{error};

    /// One wire frame. Text frames carry UTF-8 (IM stream protocols are
    /// text-frame JSON); the pipe is honest about the wire shape.
    variant frame {
        text(string),
        binary(list<u8>),
    }

    resource connection {
        /// Connect to a ws(s) URL. Async since 0.7.0: the TCP/TLS connect
        /// and the upgrade handshake are what this call waits for, and
        /// waiting is no longer a parameter.
        connect: static async func(url: string) -> result<connection, error>;

        /// Send one frame. Awaiting the call means the frame was WRITTEN to
        /// the socket, not merely queued — an ack that has been sent stays
        /// sent even if the process exits immediately after (the dingtalk
        /// ack lesson), and an await says that as precisely as the old
        /// blocking call did.
        send: async func(frame: frame) -> result<_, error>;

        /// The frames the peer sends, as they arrive. The host pings every
        /// 30s and closes the connection after 60s without any inbound frame
        /// or pong; that close ends the stream and the future's error names
        /// the reason. Reconnect and catch-up are the component's job.
        receive: func() -> tuple<stream<frame>, future<result<_, error>>>;

        /// The frames that have arrived since the last call, without
        /// waiting — for the guests that CANNOT await.
        ///
        /// A bridge's inbound pump runs inside a SYNCHRONOUS probe
        /// (docs/im-channels.md, 同步 guest 模型下的入站泵): a sync-lowered
        /// export cannot await a stream read at all, and it cannot appoint a
        /// task to do it either — a task spawned from a synchronously
        /// lowered export is never polled (measured twice:
        /// docs/wit-redesign.md section 5, legs 1b/1c, and again for this
        /// decision, with a positive control from an async export that did
        /// run). The queue is therefore drainable in the only shape such a
        /// guest has, a call.
        ///
        /// Same answer `host.subscribe` got, for the same reason: a stream
        /// hands the pace to the other end, and this guest cannot wait in
        /// the first place.
        ///
        /// One consumer per connection: `receive` and `poll` split nothing —
        /// whichever is called second gets `invalid`. An empty list means
        /// "nothing yet"; a connection whose actor is gone fails here, with
        /// the frames that did arrive delivered first (they are not
        /// discarded to report the end).
        poll: func() -> result<list<frame>, error>;
    }
}

/// Webhook ingress: the LISTEN capability for bridge components
/// (IM webhook platforms -- WhatsApp Business Cloud, 企业微信 callback;
/// docs/im-channels.md). WASI p2 has no listen, so the host runs the
/// HTTP server and pushes each request into the component's
/// `ingress-handler` export; the export's return value IS the HTTP response
/// (the platform's ack / synchronous reply). The host is a pipe:
/// method/path/headers/body pass through untouched, signature verification
/// against platform secrets is the component's job (it holds the secret;
/// the host never does). The listen ADDRESS is host config (CLI --ingress),
/// not a per-component grant.
interface ingress {
    use types.{error};

    /// A registered route. A resource since 0.7.0: dropping it stops
    /// serving the route, so "close a route that was never registered"
    /// (an error 0.6.0 had to define) is not expressible.
    resource registration;

    /// Register `route` (an absolute path, e.g. "/im/whatsapp") on the
    /// host's listen address (CLI --ingress). One registration per route;
    /// a duplicate errors instead of shadowing.
    listen: func(route: string) -> result<registration, error>;
}

/// The push half of webhook ingress: the host calls handle-request once
/// per inbound HTTP request on a registered route, under the instance lock
/// (a component mid-tool-call queues the webhook — platforms retry, that is
/// a fact of the platform, not a loss). The host still serializes per
/// instance; `async` since 0.7.0 means a handler that needs to call out
/// (http, process) awaits in place instead of parking a host thread for the
/// length of the platform's timeout. The returned response is written back
/// verbatim. A bridge with no webhook leg still exports this (the 0.3.0
/// world makes it mandatory) with a stub returning 501 — nothing calls it
/// unless the component listened.
interface ingress-handler {
    /// One inbound HTTP request.
    record request {
        /// The route that matched (the listen() argument).
        route: string,
        /// Uppercase method verb, passed through verbatim.
        method: string,
        /// The request path (== route today; a route is an exact path —
        /// prefix matching is a future contract item, not guessed here).
        path: string,
        /// The raw query string exactly as received (no leading `?`,
        /// empty when absent) — NOT parsed: percent-decoding, repeated
        /// keys and pair order are the component's semantics (signature
        /// schemes like wecom's msg_signature live in the query, and
        /// the host is a pipe).
        query: string,
        /// Headers as received (lower-cased names by the HTTP layer).
        headers: list<tuple<string, string>>,
        /// Raw body bytes.
        body: list<u8>,
    }

    /// The HTTP response to write back.
    record response {
        status: u16,
        headers: list<tuple<string, string>>,
        body: list<u8>,
    }

    handle-request: async func(request: request) -> response;
}


/// General extension: tools and/or probes, plus the host channel.
///
/// Ambient WASI (fs/env/stdio/args/network) is granted, always, and since
/// 0.8.0 there is no --deny-wasi and no per-capability gate: a component
/// runs with the permissions of the tau process. Installing/trusting its
/// signed bytes is the authorization act; the declaration shown there is
/// its imports and exports, read from the component type. Signing says
/// which component this is, not what it may do -- if a boundary is wanted,
/// it is an OS-level one around the tau process.
world extension {
    import host;
    export tools;
    export probes;
}

/// Bridge components translate an external tool protocol (MCP today) into
/// tau tools. The host knows nothing about that protocol; it only provides
/// the capabilities the bridge declares (process spawn, http, ws, ingress)
/// and those are not gated at call time since 0.8.0 -- the world it was
/// installed as IS the declaration.
///
/// Bridge components are long-lived protocol translators (MCP, IM
/// platforms). Since 0.3.0 (docs/im-channels.md contract amendment) the
/// world carries the full IM three-leg set: network (process/http/ws)
/// + the host channel (steer/follow-up inbound injection)
/// + probes (after_response outbound observation; a bridge with nothing
/// to observe returns an empty points() list). Later on the same 0.3.0
/// train (docs/im-channels.md ingress section): webhook platforms get
/// import ingress + export ingress-handler (host pushes each inbound
/// request in; the return value is the HTTP response; a bridge without a
/// webhook leg exports a 501 stub).
world bridge {
    import process;
    import http;
    import ws;
    import host;
    import ingress;
    export tools;
    export probes;
    export bridge-io;
    export ingress-handler;
}
